id: recipe.service-debugging
kind: recipe
title: Debugging a service end to end
aliases: debug a service, troubleshooting service, why is it slow
question: a service in production is slow or failing -- how do you debug it?
tags: operations, diagnostics, incident
properties:
  - symptoms separated from causes
  - layers ruled out in order
  - every conclusion tied to evidence
  - the fix followed by a prevention
invariant: Work from the outside in and from the aggregate to the specific: symptoms first, then the layer that owns them, then the single request that demonstrates it.
answer_30s: Confirm the symptom with metrics, find which layer moved (traffic, service latency, dependency latency, resource saturation), then reproduce one request and follow it through logs and a trace.
flow: |
  symptom (alert / report)
     |
  metrics: rate, errors, duration, saturation (per route, per dependency)
     |
  scope: one replica? one route? one dependency? all traffic?
     |
  evidence: logs with ids, one trace of a slow request, describe/events for the object
     |
  cause -> mitigation -> prevention (alert, test, guardrail)
compose:
  - design.observability => which signal answers which question
  - design.timeouts => distinguishing a slow dependency from a slow service
  - design.health-checking => readiness and liveness behaviour during the incident
  - k8s.debugging-workflow => the Kubernetes layer of the same procedure
  - k8s.logs => per-container and previous-instance logs
  - k8s.describe => spec, status and events for one object
  - k8s.top-resource-usage => usage against requests and limits
  - k8s.endpoints => is traffic reaching anything at all
  - rust.tracing => correlating a request across services
  - design.backpressure => recognising saturation rather than a slow component
failure_modes:
  - starting from a hypothesis and looking for evidence that supports it
  - restarting before reading the state that explains the failure
  - treating a symptom (restart everything) as the fix, so it recurs
  - no correlation id, so logs across services cannot be joined
follow_ups:
  - how would you tell a slow database from a slow network from a slow service?
related:
  - design.observability
  - k8s.debugging-workflow
  - recipe.incident-diagnosis
sources:
  - https://sre.google/sre-book/monitoring-distributed-systems/
